---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import SectionLabel from '../components/SectionLabel.astro';
import CtaSection from '../components/CtaSection.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import postItPlanning from '../assets/images/post-it-planning.jpg';
---

<Layout title="About — beCamp">
  <Header />
  <main>
    <PageHero wide label="The Story of Cville's Tech Unconference" title="How a backyard meetup became beCamp" />

    <div class="divide-y divide-divider px-5 sm:px-20">
      <section class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-10 py-20 lg:grid-cols-[480px_1fr] lg:gap-20">
        <div>
          <SectionLabel>Genesis</SectionLabel>
          <h2 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-medium tracking-[-0.36px] text-white">
            It started with Foo Camp
          </h2>
        </div>
        <div class="flex max-w-[720px] flex-col gap-6">
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-white/80">
            In 2003, Tim O'Reilly held an invitation-only conference on the future of
            technology called Foo Camp, built on the Open Space Technology approach: a
            simple way to run productive meetings for anywhere from five to 2,000+ people.
          </p>
          <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-l-[3px] border-primary pl-6">
            <p class="font-mono text-[15px] font-medium text-primary">
              Open Space runs on four principles:
            </p>
            <ul class="flex flex-col gap-2 text-base text-white">
              <li>• Whoever comes is the right people</li>
              <li>• Whatever happens is the only thing that could've</li>
              <li>• When it starts is the right time</li>
              <li>• When it's over, it's over</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-white/80">
            And one rule of movement: the Law of Two Feet, meaning anyone's free to walk
            to whatever discussion they actually care about.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-white/80">
            Foo Camp was invite-only. In 2005, BarCamp launched in Palo Alto as its open,
            anyone-welcome counterpart.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-10 py-20 lg:grid-cols-[480px_1fr] lg:gap-20">
        <div>
          <SectionLabel>Local Roots</SectionLabel>
          <h2 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-medium tracking-[-0.36px] text-white">
            Meanwhile, in Charlottesville
          </h2>
        </div>
        <div class="flex max-w-[720px] flex-col gap-6">
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-white/80">
            Around the same time, a group of UVA employees started a meetup called beTech,
            trying to bring one technologist at a time into a more modern software
            mindset. UVA's developers were largely siloed then, and this group often met
            during the workday instead of after.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-white/80">
            beCamp broke off from beTech, inspired by BarCamp. The first one happened in
            2006, in the backyard of local technologist Steve Stedman. Up until 2018's
            rebrand, beTech's logo was the only thing left connecting the two.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-white/80">
            From 2007 until becamp.org existed, beCamp ran off a wiki hosted at
            barcamp.org/becamp.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-10 py-20 lg:grid-cols-[480px_1fr] lg:gap-20">
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-6">
          <div>
            <SectionLabel>Philosophy</SectionLabel>
            <h2 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-medium tracking-[-0.36px] text-white">
              Sharing makes it better
            </h2>
          </div>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-white/80">
            Former organizer Ron DuPlain describes two rules that keep a room running
            well:
          </p>
          <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 font-mono text-sm text-link">
            <p>[1] Whatever topic the room cares about most is the one being discussed</p>
            <p>[2] Whoever has relevant perspective gets the floor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex max-w-[720px] flex-col gap-6">
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-white/80">
            The result: even a presenter who isn't the room's top expert usually gets
            useful input from someone else in the crowd, something that rarely happens at
            a traditional conference.
          </p>
          <figure class="flex flex-col gap-3">
            <Image src={postItPlanning} alt="A wall of Post-It notes forming the beCamp schedule" class="h-[360px] w-full rounded-xl object-cover" />
            <figcaption class="font-mono text-[13px] text-white/50">Planning by Post-It</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-10 py-20 lg:grid-cols-[480px_1fr] lg:gap-20">
        <div>
          <SectionLabel>Iteration</SectionLabel>
          <h2 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-medium tracking-[-0.36px] text-white">
            An evolving format
          </h2>
        </div>
        <div class="flex max-w-[720px] flex-col gap-6">
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-white/80">
            beCamp keeps iterating. Some ideas stick, some don't. The Present, Learn,
            Share labels on pitched topics came from Ron DuPlain, based on two years of
            retrospective feedback, after realizing an up-vote alone didn't guarantee a
            good session; sometimes no one in the room was an expert, sometimes a
            "presenter" actually wanted a discussion instead.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-white/80">
            beCamp has also spawned its own offshoots. Many people wanted a beCamp more
            than once a year, but twice a year was too much given the planning involved.
            Ron DuPlain started beSwarm in 2010 with John Chapin, first held that
            February; it's the spirit of beCamp's Friday night, minus the pitches, run on
            a Saturday.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-white/80">
            beSwarm led to beCraft in 2012, a software-craft meetup Stuart Leitch started
            from emails collected at that year's beSwarm, with early help from Al
            Tenhundfeld and later Cushing Whitney. It no longer meets, but it inspired
            several other tech meetups still running today.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-white/80">
            The Cville Slack team also traces back to beCamp: it began at the 2015
            retrospective, when the group decided to migrate its existing IRC community to
            Slack. That community now has over 1,000 members.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-10 py-20 lg:grid-cols-[480px_1fr] lg:gap-20">
        <div>
          <SectionLabel>Impact</SectionLabel>
          <h2 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-medium tracking-[-0.36px] text-white">
            A community of practice
          </h2>
        </div>
        <div class="flex max-w-[720px] flex-col gap-6">
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-white/80">
            beCamp stays a single annual event, but the community that runs it also backs
            several meetups and gatherings throughout the year. beCamp and beSwarm both
            grew out of tech, but the mission driving them, collaboration and inclusivity,
            reaches well past technology alone.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <CtaSection />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
